## 1. The project in plain words

**What is it?** Imagine an online store that ships thousands of orders a day. Some of them arrive late. A late order means an unhappy customer, a refund request, a support call, and sometimes a parcel that is sent back. The store would love to know *which orders are likely to be late at the moment the order is placed*, so it can act early: confirm the address, upgrade the dispatch, warn the customer, or simply keep an eye on it.

**RTO Shield AI – Model 1** is a machine-learning prototype that does exactly that:

1. It looks only at information available **before dispatch** (shipping mode, payment type, order value, time of order, destination, customer history, and so on).
2. It outputs a **risk score** between 0% and 100% — the estimated chance that the order will be delivered late.
3. It puts the order into one of three **risk tiers** (Low / Medium / High) and **flags** the High tier for attention.
4. It explains **why**, in plain English ("Shipping mode: Second Class raises risk").
5. Around the model there is a **Streamlit dashboard** with ten pages: score a single order, browse real unseen orders, simulate the money impact, score a whole CSV file, inspect model quality, read the explanations, and see exactly where the model is weak.

**What is it *not*?** This is an academic prototype built on a **public** dataset (DataCo Smart Supply Chain). The thing it predicts is `Late_delivery_risk` — whether a delivery was late. It does **not** predict Return-To-Origin (RTO), doorstep refusal, or Cash-on-Delivery default, and **no number in this repository measures RTO or Indian COD performance**. The name "RTO Shield" describes the long-term goal (a later model on real courier data); the model in this repository is the first, proof-of-concept step. A banner on every dashboard page repeats this so nobody misreads the numbers.

