## 2. What problem does it solve, and how?

### The problem

Late deliveries are costly, but intervening on *every* order is also costly (calls, messages, expedited handling). A business needs to **focus its limited attention on the orders most likely to go wrong** — without annoying the many orders that will be fine.

### The approach

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| **Predict** | A LightGBM model reads 27 pre-dispatch features and gives a raw risk score. | Pre-dispatch only: nothing that is learned *after* shipping is allowed in (no "actual days taken", no "delivery status"). |
| **Calibrate** | An isotonic calibrator turns the raw score into an honest probability: "orders scored ~70% are late about 70% of the time". | A score you can read as a probability is a score a business can price decisions on. |
| **Tier** | Two cutoffs split orders into Low / Medium / High. The cutoffs sit in *low-density gaps* of the score distribution, so tiny changes do not reshuffle orders. | Stable, explainable triage instead of an arbitrary "0.5". |
| **Flag** | Only the **High** tier is flagged. | A clear, simple operational rule: "flagged = High tier". |
| **Explain** | SHAP values are grouped into business factors and turned into sentences ("Shipping mode: Same Day raises risk"). | Every score comes with reasons a non-technical person can read. |
| **Audit** | Ablations, backtests, label-shuffle tests, per-shipping-mode checks and a verification study run automatically. | The project tells you where it is strong *and* where it is no better than a coin flip. |

### Design principles

- **Honest over impressive.** The dashboard has a Limitations page and the numbers include confidence intervals. Where the model has almost no signal (Standard Class and Second Class shipping), the app says so on the page where you score an order.
- **Time-respecting evaluation.** Orders are split **chronologically** (train on the past, validate on the next slice, test on the most recent slice). No random shuffling, so no peeking into the future.
- **One source of truth.** Every metric is computed once (step 10) into `outputs/final_metrics.json` and `models/thresholds.json`. The dashboard, this README and the Model Card read from those files; nothing is typed by hand.
- **Real data only.** No simulated orders anywhere in the app. The "example orders" are real held-out orders.

