### 📊 Performance — how good is the model, really?

**Purpose:** all the evaluation evidence, split into five tabs.

| Tab | What it shows |
|---|---|
| **Model comparison** | Four lead cards (ROC-AUC with CI, PR-AUC vs. late-rate baseline, High-tier precision and coverage, calibration error before → after) plus a chart and table comparing the baseline logistic regression, full logistic regression, random forest, XGBoost and the final tuned LightGBM on the validation and test sets. |
| **Curves** (ROC & PR) | Interactive ROC and precision-recall curves, computed from **raw scores**. |
| **Calibration** | Reliability diagram and table: predicted probability vs. the observed late rate, before and after isotonic calibration, with ECE. |
| **Lift & gain** | Decile table — if you sorted orders by risk and looked at the top 10%, how many times more late orders would you find than at random (lift), and what share of all late orders do the top X% capture (gain)? |
| **Risk tiers & policies** | The tier table (share of orders, actual late rate and precision per tier), the **policy table** (flag-High vs. other thresholds, etc.), and the **threshold-stability** table showing how little the flagged share moves if you shift a cutoff by ±0.01 or ±0.02. |

---

### 🔍 Explainability — why does the model score what it scores?

**Purpose:** global explanations from SHAP (a method that splits a prediction into per-feature contributions).

Three tabs:

- **Global importance:** which *business factors* matter most on average (shipping mode, scheduled days, payment type, etc.), grouped so that all one-hot columns of one factor are added together.
- **Beeswarm:** every dot is one order; position shows whether the feature pushed the score up or down, colour shows the feature's value.
- **SHAP dependence (5 continuous features):** one sub-tab per numeric feature (e.g. order hour, order value, scheduled days) showing how the contribution changes across its range.

A blue "How to read this" box at the top explains the plots in plain language.

---

### ⚙️ Robustness — does it hold up under stress?

**Purpose:** five tabs of stress tests. A red callout at the top states the headline weakness (Standard and Second Class).

| Tab | Question answered |
|---|---|
| **By shipping mode** | Is the model equally good for each shipping mode? (ROC-AUC with CI and an "informative / limited" flag per mode.) |
| **Feature ablation** | What if we remove a group of features? Do the **history features** (customer and country past behaviour) actually help? (They add no statistically significant gain.) |
| **Temporal backtest** | If we trained on earlier periods and tested on later ones (5 expanding folds), is performance stable over time? |
| **Drift (PSI)** | Has the distribution of the main features shifted between training and test? (Population Stability Index.) |
| **Sanity checks** | A **label-shuffle** test (scramble the answers: the model must fall to ≈ 0.5 AUC, proving it isn't cheating), a **trivial-policy comparison** (flag everyone / no one / random), and the **payment-type (TRANSFER) finding** — an apparent "payment effect" that turns out to be a labelling artifact. |

---

### ⚠️ Limitations — what this model must not be used for

**Purpose:** six cards, each a plain-language caveat: (1) the proxy-target reality, (2) near rule-based label construction, (3) payment type is a label artifact, (4) the Standard / Second Class blind spot, (5) geographic scope and public-data constraints, (6) advisory-only governance and ethical guard-rails. Read this page before quoting any number.

