## 13. Honest limitations: read before you trust a number

1. **Near rule-based labelling.** `Late_delivery_risk` is a pre-computed label in the DataCo dataset that heavily reflects near rule-based dispatch logic rather than real courier variance. For Same Day orders, the order hour alone separates outcomes: orders placed before 12:00 are late {{ M.same_day_noon_rule.late_rate_before_noon | pct1 }} of the time, orders from 12:00 onward {{ M.same_day_noon_rule.late_rate_from_noon | pct1 }} ({{ M.same_day_noon_rule.orders | int }} Same Day orders).
2. **Standard Class has almost no signal.** Standard Class is {{ M.per_mode[mode=Standard Class].share | pct1 }} of test orders, and the model's ROC-AUC there is **{{ M.per_mode[mode=Standard Class].roc_auc | f4 }}** (95% CI {{ M.per_mode[mode=Standard Class].roc_auc_ci_lo | f4 }} to {{ M.per_mode[mode=Standard Class].roc_auc_ci_hi | f4 }}; signal: **{{ M.per_mode[mode=Standard Class].flag | raw }}**). The aggregate test ROC-AUC ({{ M.test.roc_auc | f4 }}) is driven by First Class ({{ M.per_mode[mode=First Class].roc_auc | f4 }}) and Same Day ({{ M.per_mode[mode=Same Day].roc_auc | f4 }}). A model trained on Standard Class alone reaches only {{ M.verification.segment_models.Standard Class.segment_model_roc_auc | f4 }}.
3. **The payment-type (TRANSFER) effect is a labelling artifact.** All {{ M.verification.cancel_fraud_orders | int }} CANCELED / SUSPECTED_FRAUD orders are TRANSFER payments and every one of them carries `Late_delivery_risk = 0`. Within each shipping mode the TRANSFER late rate differs from other payment types by {{ M.verification.transfer_gap_within_mode_all | pp1 }}; with those orders removed the difference is {{ M.verification.transfer_gap_within_mode_clean | pp1 }}. Do not read TRANSFER as lower payment risk.
4. **The target is a proxy.** It measures *late delivery*, not RTO, doorstep refusal or COD default. No result here measures RTO or Indian COD performance.
5. **Not validated on Indian D2C.** This dataset represents global supply-chain orders (predominantly North America and LATAM). It does not capture Indian D2C last-mile dynamics (NDR verification, fake door attempts, PIN-code delivery tiers, or COD payment fraud). Do not deploy without retraining on actual courier and return telemetry.
6. **Scores are clustered.** The calibrated score takes only {{ M.score_clusters.unique_scores_test | int }} distinct values on the test set; {{ M.score_clusters.largest_standard_cluster_share_of_standard | pct1 }} of Standard Class orders share one score. Cutoffs are therefore placed in low-density gaps between clusters.
7. **Advisory use only.** The score should support human triage. It must not be used to refuse service, discriminate between customers or make automated adverse decisions.
8. **Impact numbers are what-ifs.** The Impact Simulator uses user-entered costs and success rates; the dataset has no record of real interventions.

**Payment-type proxy.** Every CANCELED / SUSPECTED_FRAUD order is a TRANSFER payment labelled on-time, so the payment features (`Type`, `is_cash`) partly proxy post-dispatch order status (which is never a model input). Dropping them lowers test ROC-AUC from {{ M.type_audit.all_orders.model_a.roc_auc | f4 }} to {{ M.type_audit.all_orders.model_b.roc_auc | f4 }} on all orders (difference {{ M.type_audit.all_orders.delta_roc_auc.point | s4 }}, 95% CI {{ M.type_audit.all_orders.delta_roc_auc.ci | ci4 }}), but only from {{ M.type_audit.cleaned_orders.model_a.roc_auc | f4 }} to {{ M.type_audit.cleaned_orders.model_b.roc_auc | f4 }} on the {{ M.type_audit.cleaned_orders.n | int }} cleaned orders where cancelled/fraud orders are removed (difference {{ M.type_audit.cleaned_orders.delta_roc_auc.point | s4 }}, 95% CI {{ M.type_audit.cleaned_orders.delta_roc_auc.ci | ci4 }}, which includes zero). At equal coverage (the same {{ M.type_audit.equal_coverage.all_orders.k | int }} orders flagged, {{ M.type_audit.equal_coverage.all_orders.flagged_share | pct1 }}), High-tier precision is {{ M.type_audit.equal_coverage.all_orders.model_a.precision | pct1 }} with the payment features versus {{ M.type_audit.equal_coverage.all_orders.model_b.precision | pct1 }} without on all orders, and {{ M.type_audit.equal_coverage.cleaned_orders.model_a.precision | pct1 }} versus {{ M.type_audit.equal_coverage.cleaned_orders.model_b.precision | pct1 }} on cleaned orders. The gain from payment type is therefore consistent with a labelling artifact, not a real payment risk. The model with payment features stays the primary model; every payment-type reason code and chart label in the app carries a dagger (†) with the footnote "Payment-type effects reflect how DataCo labels cancelled/fraud orders (all TRANSFER, all on time), not a real payment risk."

